<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Council of Clermont</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1095">1095</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Council of Clermont was a church council held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-urban-ii">Pope Urban II</a> at Clermont in Auvergne in November 1095. Besides decrees on church reform, it ended with Urban's sermon of 27 November calling on western Christians to aid the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> and free <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, which launched the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a>. The crowd is said to have answered with the cry 'Deus vult' ('God wills it').</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/council-of-clermont.jpg" alt="Council of Clermont" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jean Colombe · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Passages_d%27outremer_Fr5594,_fol._19r,_Concile_de_Clermont.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Byzantine emperor Alexius I Comnenus had sent envoys to the Council of Piacenza asking for military assistance against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Turks</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-urban-ii">Pope Urban II</a> reacted to that request. The council that followed was a mixed synod of clergy and laymen held from 17 to 27 November 1095 at Clermont in Auvergne, then part of the Duchy of Aquitaine. About 300 French clerics attended. No official list of the participants or of the signatories to its decrees survives.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The council was above all a synod for implementing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cluniac-reforms">Cluniac reforms</a>, enacting decrees and settling local and regional issues. It extended the excommunication of Philip I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> for his adulterous remarriage to Bertrade of Montfort and renewed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-and-truce-of-god">Truce of God</a>, an attempt by the church to reduce feuding among Frankish nobles.</p><p>Urban's speech on 27 November is known from several accounts that vary widely. Fulcher of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chartres">Chartres</a>, who was present and wrote from memory around 1100 to 1105, is generally considered the most reliable. In his version Urban first urges the clergy to vigilance and calls for the restoration of order in the Frankish provinces. He then asks the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>, once peace is re-established, to aid the Christians of the East, whom the Turks had attacked and from whom they had taken Byzantine Anatolia as far west as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. He ends with the words 'Christ commands it' and promises immediate absolution to all who die on the way or in battle against Muslims.</p><p>Robert the Monk, writing in 1107, gives a more vivid sermon and has the audience burst out with the cry Deus vult. His Urban stresses the reconquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-land">Holy Land</a> more than aid to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, a theme many historians consider an insertion shaped by the success of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>In a letter of December 1095 to the faithful in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>, Urban called on the princes to free the churches of the East, named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adhemar-of-le-puy">Adhemar of Le Puy</a> as leader of the expedition and set the departure for the feast of the Assumption, 15 August 1096. The anonymous Gesta Francorum records that news of the call spread quickly through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> and that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> had crosses sewn on their right shoulders. The call led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a>, the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> and the founding of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Council of Clermont” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1095 he preached the First Crusade at the <b>Council of Clermont</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-12">12 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Closing the <b>Council of Clermont</b>, Urban II urged Western knights to aid Byzantium and free Jerusalem.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-27">27 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1095 Urban II proclaimed the First Crusade at the <b>Council of Clermont</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-auvergne">County of Auvergne</a></li></ul></section>
